---
description: A component was referenced by name, but that name is not in the grid's registered components.
---

## Component Name Not Registered

A column or grid option asked for a component by name, setting {% errorParam name="propertyName" /%} to the string {% errorParam name="componentName" /%}, and the grid could not find that name when it came to render. The cell falls back to the grid's default component.

The grid tells two cases apart here. Naming a built-in component whose module is not registered raises [error #200](./errors/200) with the module to register, so arriving at #101 means the grid believes this is your own component. That leaves two causes, and the message's `Did you mean` suggestions usually settle which:

- A typo, or a mismatch in case. Names are matched exactly, so `myCellRenderer` and `MyCellRenderer` are different components.
- The component was never registered, or was registered on a different grid than the one rendering this column.

### Register it by name

{% if isFramework("javascript", "react", "angular") %}
Names are resolved from the `components` grid option. The name in the map must match the string on the column exactly.

```js
import { MyCellRenderer } from './MyCellRenderer';
```

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    components: {
        MyCellRenderer: MyCellRenderer,
    },
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'status', cellRenderer: 'MyCellRenderer' },
    ],
}
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("vue") %}
Vue resolves the name through Vue's own registries rather than the grid's `components` option. Register the component globally with `app.component`, locally through the enclosing component's `components` option, or expose it with `defineExpose` when using `script setup`:

```js
import MyCellRenderer from './MyCellRenderer.vue';

app.component('MyCellRenderer', MyCellRenderer);
```

The grid's `components` option still has an effect in Vue, but it maps one name to another rather than a name to a component, so whatever it points at has to be registered with Vue as well.
{% /if %}

See [Registering Custom Components](./components/#registering-custom-components) for the full set of forms.

### Or skip the name

Referencing the component directly avoids this class of error, because a wrong name becomes a compile-time or import error rather than something found at render:

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'status', cellRenderer: MyCellRenderer },
    ],
}
```

Registering by name is still worth it where the name has to come from configuration or data, such as a saved layout or a server response, since a string is all you have in that case.

### If several grids need it

The `components` option applies to one grid, and a grid that defines its own map does not see another grid's. To make a component available to every grid, register it through [Global Grid Options](./grid-interface/#global-grid-options) with the `deep` merge strategy, so a grid's own map is merged with the global one rather than replacing it. All component types share one registry, so names must be unique across it.
